1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">"Self-driving policies learned by behavioral
cloning are usually trained open-loop,</font>

2
00:00:05,828 --> 00:00:08,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching expert actions one step at a time,</font>

3
00:00:08,589 --> 00:00:13,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet they are deployed closed-loop where every
action shapes future states.</font>

4
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:16,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">This mismatch quietly hurts real-world safety.</font>

5
00:00:16,293 --> 00:00:18,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces C-W-E-R-M,</font>

6
00:00:18,347 --> 00:00:23,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">Closed-loop Weighted Empirical Risk Minimization: a
simple two-stage recipe that first</font>

7
00:00:23,868 --> 00:00:27,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">runs a policy in a simulator to find the scenes
where it fails,</font>

8
00:00:27,912 --> 00:00:32,134
<font color="#FFFFFF">then upsamples exactly those scenes when training
the final policy.</font>

9
00:00:32,214 --> 00:00:36,756
<font color="#FFFFFF">On a challenging urban driving dataset it cuts
collisions substantially,</font>

10
00:00:36,836 --> 00:00:40,672
<font color="#FFFFFF">with improvements around thirty-five percent on
some metrics,</font>

11
00:00:40,752 --> 00:00:45,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">all without a differentiable simulator or costly
closed-loop training."</font>

12
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:50,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">"Imitation learning for self-driving cars is
usually done through behavioral cloning,</font>

13
00:00:50,998 --> 00:00:55,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the network is trained to reproduce an
expert's next action.</font>

14
00:00:55,181 --> 00:01:01,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">The catch is that this is done open-loop: the model
never sees the consequences of its own actions.</font>

15
00:01:01,457 --> 00:01:03,659
<font color="#FFFFFF">But when the policy actually drives,</font>

16
00:01:03,739 --> 00:01:06,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">every action changes the future state it will see.</font>

17
00:01:06,908 --> 00:01:09,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">Small prediction errors accumulate,</font>

18
00:01:09,126 --> 00:01:14,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">pushing the car into out-of-distribution situations
the model was never trained on.</font>

19
00:01:14,388 --> 00:01:19,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the metrics that truly matter, like collisions,
are non-differentiable,</font>

20
00:01:19,142 --> 00:01:23,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they are effectively invisible to the standard
training loss.</font>

21
00:01:23,198 --> 00:01:29,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a policy that looks great open-loop
but drives poorly in closed-loop evaluation."</font>

22
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,366
<font color="#FFFFFF">"There have been attempts to close this gap.</font>

23
00:01:32,446 --> 00:01:38,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">Methods like Urban Driver run a differentiable
simulator directly inside the training</font>

24
00:01:38,098 --> 00:01:40,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">loop using backpropagation through time.</font>

25
00:01:40,758 --> 00:01:45,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">This works, but it is expensive: it requires a
differentiable simulator,</font>

26
00:01:45,545 --> 00:01:46,995
<font color="#FFFFFF">it does not scale well,</font>

27
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:51,982
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it carries the heavy memory cost of unrolling
policies during training.</font>

28
00:01:52,062 --> 00:01:56,437
<font color="#FFFFFF">Other approaches collect on-policy data or add
extra human oracles,</font>

29
00:01:56,517 --> 00:01:58,166
<font color="#FFFFFF">which are slow and costly.</font>

30
00:01:58,246 --> 00:02:04,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ask a simpler question: can we get
closed-loop benefits by only using a simulator</font>

31
00:02:04,430 --> 00:02:06,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">to decide which training scenes matter,</font>

32
00:02:07,023 --> 00:02:11,332
<font color="#FFFFFF">without changing the loss function or requiring
differentiability?</font>

33
00:02:11,412 --> 00:02:13,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">That question motivates CW-ERM."</font>

34
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:17,513
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The paper makes three contributions.</font>

35
00:02:17,593 --> 00:02:21,265
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it proposes Closed-loop Weighted Empirical
Risk Minimization,</font>

36
00:02:21,345 --> 00:02:25,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">a technique that leverages closed-loop metrics from
policy rollouts to debias the policy</font>

37
00:02:25,098 --> 00:02:28,771
<font color="#FFFFFF">network and shrink the distribution gap between
open-loop training and closed-loop inference.</font>

38
00:02:28,851 --> 00:02:32,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

39
00:02:32,604 --> 00:02:36,276
<font color="#FFFFFF">it evaluates the method experimentally on a
challenging urban driving dataset and shows</font>

40
00:02:36,356 --> 00:02:40,029
<font color="#FFFFFF">significant closed-loop improvements,</font>

41
00:02:40,109 --> 00:02:43,782
<font color="#FFFFFF">all without complex or computationally expensive
closed-loop training.</font>

42
00:02:43,862 --> 00:02:47,535
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

43
00:02:47,615 --> 00:02:51,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">it establishes an important theoretical connection
between this reweighting scheme and</font>

44
00:02:51,367 --> 00:02:55,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">the classic family of methods that correct
covariate shift through density-ratio estimation."</font>

45
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:59,366
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The method has three stages and is strikingly simple.</font>

46
00:02:59,446 --> 00:03:03,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">In stage one, you train an identification policy
the ordinary way,</font>

47
00:03:03,473 --> 00:03:07,419
<font color="#FFFFFF">with standard ERM behavioral cloning.</font>

48
00:03:07,499 --> 00:03:11,445
<font color="#FFFFFF">In stage two,</font>

49
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:15,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">you take that policy and roll it out in a
closed-loop simulator over the training scenes,</font>

50
00:03:15,551 --> 00:03:19,498
<font color="#FFFFFF">collecting metrics like the number of collisions.</font>

51
00:03:19,578 --> 00:03:23,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every scene where a cost is positive, meaning the
policy failed,</font>

52
00:03:23,604 --> 00:03:27,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">goes into an error set.</font>

53
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:31,576
<font color="#FFFFFF">In stage three, you train the final policy again
with weighted ERM,</font>

54
00:03:31,656 --> 00:03:35,603
<font color="#FFFFFF">but now the error-set scenes are upsampled by a factor w.</font>

55
00:03:35,683 --> 00:03:39,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is nearly identical to the original
behavioral-cloning objective,</font>

56
00:03:39,709 --> 00:03:43,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the single addition of a weighting term driven
by closed-loop failures.</font>

57
00:03:43,735 --> 00:03:47,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two practical tricks matter: the identification
policy is stopped early so the error set is not depleted,</font>

58
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:51,708
<font color="#FFFFFF">and upsampling is used instead of reweighting
because it is more stable.</font>

59
00:03:51,788 --> 00:03:55,734
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, because the simulator is only used to
pick scenes,</font>

60
00:03:55,814 --> 00:03:59,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">any non-differentiable metric works and no
differentiable simulator is needed."</font>

61
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:04,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The method is evaluated on a proprietary
real-world dataset,</font>

62
00:04:04,830 --> 00:04:09,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">collected from the company's self-driving vehicles
on challenging urban missions in San Francisco and Palo Alto.</font>

63
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:14,130
<font color="#FFFFFF">It includes recorded trajectories of the ego
vehicle and surrounding agents together with high-definition maps.</font>

64
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:18,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">The scenarios are diverse and difficult: stopping
behind a lead vehicle,</font>

65
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:23,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">stopping at intersections, and navigating dense
traffic with cars, pedestrians,</font>

66
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:28,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">and cyclists.</font>

67
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:32,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">Most scenes run eleven to thirteen seconds, with
the longest reaching thirty seconds.</font>

68
00:04:32,970 --> 00:04:37,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">In total,</font>

69
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:42,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors train on one hundred and eighty hours
of driving and validate and test on sixty hours each.</font>

70
00:04:42,350 --> 00:04:46,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also open-source the closed-loop simulator and
metrics used in the work."</font>

71
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:48,858
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The results are strong.</font>

72
00:04:48,938 --> 00:04:54,252
<font color="#FFFFFF">Compared against the best baseline, behavioral
cloning with ERM and perturbation,</font>

73
00:04:54,332 --> 00:04:58,047
<font color="#FFFFFF">CW-ERM significantly reduces collisions across the board,</font>

74
00:04:58,127 --> 00:05:02,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">with improvements reaching about thirty-five
percent on some metrics.</font>

75
00:05:02,722 --> 00:05:05,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">When the model upsamples front-collision scenes,</font>

76
00:05:05,918 --> 00:05:08,768
<font color="#FFFFFF">front collisions fall from fourteen to nine.</font>

77
00:05:08,848 --> 00:05:11,432
<font color="#FFFFFF">When it upsamples side-collision scenes,</font>

78
00:05:11,512 --> 00:05:14,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">side collisions fall from fifty-five to forty-seven.</font>

79
00:05:14,974 --> 00:05:20,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">There is a nice side effect too: improving one
metric often improves related ones.</font>

80
00:05:20,435 --> 00:05:24,084
<font color="#FFFFFF">Upsampling side collisions also reduces rear collisions,</font>

81
00:05:24,164 --> 00:05:30,077
<font color="#FFFFFF">evidence that the policy is becoming less passive
rather than just gaming a single number.</font>

82
00:05:30,157 --> 00:05:35,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">Variance is also lower than the baseline in several
cases, so the gains are stable."</font>

83
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:39,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The ablations reveal how to use the method well.</font>

84
00:05:39,209 --> 00:05:44,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">Targeting a single metric in isolation gives the
largest improvement on that metric,</font>

85
00:05:44,626 --> 00:05:50,026
<font color="#FFFFFF">while combining metrics achieves a balance, tracing
out a Pareto front of trade-offs.</font>

86
00:05:50,106 --> 00:05:54,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combining front, side, and distance-to-reference
works nicely,</font>

87
00:05:54,104 --> 00:05:57,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">but adding rear collisions causes a clear regression.</font>

88
00:05:57,521 --> 00:06:02,019
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors trace this to false positives in the
rear-collision metric,</font>

89
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:06,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">caused by log-replayed agents that do not react in
the simulator.</font>

90
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:08,273
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two hyperparameters also matter.</font>

91
00:06:08,353 --> 00:06:14,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">The early-stopping budget K of the identification
policy: ten epochs works best for single-metric</font>

92
00:06:14,608 --> 00:06:17,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">error sets and twenty for multi-metric.</font>

93
00:06:17,122 --> 00:06:22,072
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the upsampling factor: performance improves up
to about a factor of fifty,</font>

94
00:06:22,152 --> 00:06:25,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">after which side collisions start to rise again,</font>

95
00:06:25,247 --> 00:06:29,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">echoing a similar saturation observed in the Just
Train Twice method."</font>

96
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:36,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">"To put concrete numbers on it: front collisions
drop from fourteen to nine against the strongest baseline,</font>

97
00:06:37,035 --> 00:06:39,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">roughly a thirty-six percent reduction.</font>

98
00:06:39,577 --> 00:06:44,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">Distance-to-reference-trajectory failures fall from
thirty-five to twenty-eight,</font>

99
00:06:44,792 --> 00:06:46,863
<font color="#FFFFFF">about a twenty percent reduction.</font>

100
00:06:46,943 --> 00:06:50,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">Side collisions go from fifty-five to forty-seven.</font>

101
00:06:50,202 --> 00:06:55,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">Overall the paper reports improvements of around
thirty-five percent on some metrics.</font>

102
00:06:55,742 --> 00:06:58,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">The best upsampling factor is around fifty.</font>

103
00:06:58,545 --> 00:07:03,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">The models were trained on one hundred and eighty
hours of driving data,</font>

104
00:07:03,239 --> 00:07:05,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">validated and tested on sixty hours each,</font>

105
00:07:05,911 --> 00:07:08,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the final policy trained for forty epochs."</font>

106
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:11,787
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The takeaway is refreshingly practical.</font>

107
00:07:11,867 --> 00:07:15,829
<font color="#FFFFFF">You do not need a differentiable simulator, a human
in the loop,</font>

108
00:07:15,909 --> 00:07:19,746
<font color="#FFFFFF">or expensive closed-loop training to get
closed-loop benefits.</font>

109
00:07:19,826 --> 00:07:24,420
<font color="#FFFFFF">You just need to run your policy once in a
simulator, note where it fails,</font>

110
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:26,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">upsample those scenes, and retrain.</font>

111
00:07:26,711 --> 00:07:33,074
<font color="#FFFFFF">That simple recipe delivers significant reductions
in collisions and other non-differentiable metrics,</font>

112
00:07:33,154 --> 00:07:37,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">works with any closed-loop metric, and adds no
inference latency.</font>

113
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:43,054
<font color="#FFFFFF">And it comes with a clean theoretical story:
weighting scenes by failure is closely connected</font>

114
00:07:43,134 --> 00:07:47,034
<font color="#FFFFFF">to correcting covariate shift through density-ratio
estimation,</font>

115
00:07:47,114 --> 00:07:52,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">a promising direction for making imitation-learned
planners both simpler and safer."</font>
